  Q1 findings — let me consolidate the concrete correctness items:

  (a) `winKey` quantization to centi-Da (line 1723-1725, 2293-2294): window boundaries rounded to 0.01 Da; precursor membership uses the QUANTIZED win_lo/win_hi while fragments were routed by the TRUE precursor window. A precursor within ±5 mDa of a true edge is assigned to the wrong side — silent misassignment of a handful of precursors per run. Also two windows closer than 0.01 Da in both edges would merge (won't happen in practice). Severity: low-medium. Fix: keep double keys (e.g. round to 1e-6 or use the doubles directly in an ordered map with exact compare since they come from the same source values).

  (b) `scoreCandidates_` line 1440-1448: precursor XIC points are snapped to the NEAREST grid point within delta_rt; multiple precursor points can collapse onto one grid point and sum (`pdense[gi] += pval`), while `overlap` counts FRAGMENT support points touching nonzero pdense. Not a bug per se. Hmm.

  Actually a subtler one: `touched` tracks grid points set by THIS precursor; reset loop zeroes them. If the same gi gets contributions, fine. But `if (pdense[gi] == 0.0f) touched.push_back(gi)` — if a precursor XIC point has pval == 0 exactly... fine.

  (c) `buildFragGrid` line 728: `int gi = (int)(lower_bound(g.rt..., p.first) - g.rt.begin())` — p.first came from the same union, so exact match found; fine. `g.off[i] = (uint32_t)g.flat.size()` — narrowing: if total support points > 4.29e9, overflow. 8.5M fragments × ~20 points = 1.7e8, OK, but no guard. Minor.

  (d) `FragGrid::off` is uint32_t; `supBegin/supEnd` return size_t from uint32 — fine.

  (e) `xicCorr` rt matching greedy; fine.

  (f) inferPrecursors_ count path: `findPartner` tolerance uses seed m/z for tol but the partner window for z>=2 leftward walk: tol = s.mz*ppm — at z=5, k=5 left: target = s.mz - 5*ISO/5 ≈ s.mz - 1.003. tol relative to seed — fine (within ppm of each other).

  Real issue in count path: `used[]` marking — partners of NON-best hypotheses are not marked, but they are also not unmarked; fine. But: within the z-loop, the same partner j can be found for multiple z hypotheses; when best partners marked used, other hypotheses' candidates remain unused and can seed their own precursors — intended.

  Potential subtle bug: `findPartner` returns the FIRST unused partner in m/z order within the window, not the closest in m/z or the most intense — greedy arbitrary choice within tolerance. For the mono walk that could pick the wrong of two candidate peaks. Behavior choice, deterministic. Not a crash.

  (g) The `mono_select_` block line 1232: `mono = (best_c < nL) ? ms1[lefts[nL - 1 - best_c]].mz : s.mz;` — check indexing: runI is [lefts reversed (nL entries), seed, rights]. Candidate c means mono at runI index c. For c < nL, mono is lefts[nL-1-c] ✓ (runI[0] = lefts[nL-1] = furthest left). OK.

  (h) Envelope path: `hyps` includes hypotheses for lead 0..3; `members` excludes seed; `used` marks members of best only. Note the seed may equal a found partner iso_idx[k] (if seed is M+1 of the mono), then `if ((size_t)j != seed) members.push_back(j)` — seed excluded from members but seed is marked used anyway ✓.

  (i) n_isotopes for envelope: `1 + best.partners.size()` where partners=members (excludes seed when seed isn't mono)... if mono is at lead=2 (mono_idx != seed), members includes mono_idx and other partners but NOT seed; seed is an isotope peak of the envelope though. n_isotopes = 1+members — counts mono + members, seed excluded → undercounts by 1 when seed ≠ mono. Minor metadata inaccuracy (used by SPEXTRACT_MIN_ISOTOPES gate → could wrongly pass/fail edge cases). Low severity but it IS an off-by-one in a gate. Actually MIN_ISOTOPES is falsified/off. Still worth noting.

  (j) `atrousLevels` line 698: `nth_element(d.begin(), d.begin() + d.size()/2, d.end())` fine.

  (k) `aggregateFrames_` line 311: `const Size lo = (i > (Size)half) ? i - half : 0;` — when i == half exactly, lo = 0, but should be i-half = 0 anyway. fine. `hi = min(N-1, i+half)` — window is [i-half, i+half] inclusive = n_frames points when n_frames odd. For even n_frames, half = n/2, window is n+1 points — asymmetric but harmless.

  Cluster consumption bug candidate: inner while iterates b over ALL buf entries within mz tol of mz0, marking consumed those within im_tol of buf[a]. But entries NOT matching im remain; then `while (a < buf.size() && buf[a].in < 0.0) ++a;` advances to next unconsumed. But consumed entries between a and b that were skipped... the loop `for (size_t a = 0; ...)` — after processing cluster at a, advance a past consumed. Next unconsumed a' starts a new cluster with mz0' = buf[a'].mz, and the while (b) scan goes from a' again over remaining — but b starts at a (a'), scanning forward only — entries before a' unconsumed? Can't be: all entries < a' were consumed (we advanced past all consumed; unconsumed earlier entries would have stopped the advance). Wait — entries in [a, b) that didn't match IM are unconsumed and sit between consumed ones; the advance `while buf[a].in < 0 ++a` stops at the first unconsumed, which could be within the mz tolerance of the previous cluster — it starts a new cluster there. OK correct, O(n²) worst case within tol band but fine.

  (l) `compactify` guard `mz * MZ_Q > 4.2e9` — uint32 max is 4.2949e9; llround(mz*1e5) for mz=42949 → 4.2949e9 fits; guard at 4.2e9 conservative ✓.

  (m) `loadMzPeakStreaming` line 271: frame_id parse `id.find("frame=")` — ok. Line 299: `best->lo < -1e8 || best->hi > 1e8` rejects ions without IM band ✓ fail-closed.

